issues.problematic_code_snippet:
// Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out
await Task.Yield();

var state = await AuthenticationProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();

private async Task ProcessLogOut(string returnUrl)
{
    if (!ValidateSignOutRequestState())
    {
        Log.LogoutOperationInitiatedExternally(Logger);
        Navigation.NavigateTo(ApplicationPaths.LogOutFailedPath, AuthenticationNavigationOptions with { HistoryEntryState = "The logout was not initiated from within the page." });
        return;
    }

public void Redirect()
{
    if (_tokenResult.InteractionOptions != null)
    {
        _navigation.NavigateToLogin(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl, _tokenResult.InteractionOptions);
    }
    else
    {
        _navigation.NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl!);
    }

findings.body:
The old `ProcessLogOut` only failed on a null `HistoryEntryState` when the legacy session-storage check also failed. The new code is `if (!ValidateSignOutRequestState())`, and `ValidateSignOutRequestState()` is `GetCachedNavigationState()?.Interaction == InteractionType.SignOut`. With null state, `GetCachedNavigationState()` returns null, so validation returns false and the logout is rejected.

The PR description says the change should "only fail validation when state exists but is invalid". The code does the reverse, so either the description is wrong or the behavior is. Either way the author should decide deliberately and state it.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="279-292"}

::callout{type="warning"}
With `SignOutSessionStateManager` removed, nothing can satisfy the old null-state fallback, so "null state fails" is probably the intended end state. It still needs to be stated in the description and covered by a test, and the description needs to match the code.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="281-282"}
// Keep the description and the test in sync with this behavior:
// a null HistoryEntryState or a non-SignOut interaction is rejected.
if (!ValidateSignOutRequestState())

The old guard was `InteractionOptions != null && InteractiveRequestUrl != null`. The new guard drops the URL check, so when `InteractionOptions` is non-null but `InteractiveRequestUrl` is null, `NavigateToLogin(null, ...)` is called.

The else branch also changed. It now calls `NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl!)`, and the `!` hides a nullable value. The old code used `RedirectUrl!`, which only worked because the obsolete constructor always set it. Now a result with both values null throws from `NavigateTo` with a generic null error.

The framework's own `RequestAccessToken` always sets both values together, so the in-repo path is safe. The public four-argument constructor still accepts `(RequiresRedirect, token, null, options)`, and no test covers this combination.

